{"vid":"2z7h5BOZ2Hk","source":"2z7h5BOZ2Hk.en.json3","lang":"en","cues":[{"t":1.2,"txt":"The US Chemical Safety Board is an independent non-regulatory agency created by"},{"t":7.5,"txt":"Congress that investigates catastrophic chemical incidents and makes recommendations to prevent"},{"t":13.2,"txt":"similar events from occurring. Over its 27-year history, the CSB has deployed to nearly 180"},{"t":20.7,"txt":"major chemical incidents. Those incidents have involved more than 200 fatalities,"},{"t":26.3,"txt":"over 1,300 serious injuries, and billions of dollars in damage to property and the environment."},{"t":33.8,"txt":"The incidents have also harmed communities and caused significant business disruptions,"},{"t":39.1,"txt":"including job losses and reduced earnings for the American chemical industry. There's"},{"t":45.2,"txt":"a saying among safety experts that if you think safety is expensive, try having an accident."},{"t":51,"txt":"Chemical incidents are always costly, but several of the recent incidents investigated by the CSB"},{"t":56.6,"txt":"have resulted in incredible economic losses for chemical companies and local businesses,"},{"t":61.4,"txt":"caused deaths and serious injuries, and put nearby communities and workers at serious"},{"t":66.2,"txt":"risk. For instance, on January 24th, 2020, an accidental release of propylene accumulated and"},{"t":74,"txt":"exploded inside a building at the Watson Grinding Facility in Houston, Texas. The massive explosion"},{"t":80.9,"txt":"fatally injured two employees and a member of the public who lived nearby. Hundreds of neighboring"},{"t":86.9,"txt":"structures were damaged, including homes and several businesses. As a result of the incident,"},{"t":92.7,"txt":"Watson Grinding filed for bankruptcy, and the company is no longer in business. Approximately"},{"t":98.9,"txt":"$90 million in settlements and judgment amounts have been awarded to victims and"},{"t":104.2,"txt":"nearby residents impacted by the explosion. On November 27th, 2019, major explosions and"},{"t":112.6,"txt":"fires occurred at the TPC Group chemical plant in Port Nous, Texas, when a piping section ruptured,"},{"t":119,"txt":"releasing highly flammable butadene that quickly ignited. The resulting pressure wave destroyed"},{"t":125.6,"txt":"parts of the facility and injured two TPC employees and a security contractor. Residents"},{"t":132.4,"txt":"living within 4 miles of the plant were ordered to evacuate, and the blast reportedly was felt up"},{"t":138.2,"txt":"to 30 m away. The incident caused $450 million in on-site property damage and $153 million in damage"},{"t":148.1,"txt":"to nearby homes and businesses. The explosion also led to reduced usage of the Sabine nature's"},{"t":155.4,"txt":"waterway, the nation's third largest waterway by cargo volume and a major economic driver in"},{"t":161.8,"txt":"the United States. On June 21st, 2019, a large vapor cloud ignited at the Philadelphia Energy"},{"t":169.5,"txt":"Solutions refinery in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania, causing an enormous fire and multiple explosions."},{"t":176.5,"txt":"The explosions caused the release of over 5,000 lbs of highly toxic hydrofluoric acid"},{"t":183,"txt":"that potentially threatened the surrounding community. Additionally, a huge metal fragment"},{"t":188.6,"txt":"weighing approximately 38,000 lbs flew completely across the Skucal River and other large fragments"},{"t":195.8,"txt":"landed in the refinery. The incident caused an estimated $750 million in property damage and"},{"t":203.3,"txt":"ultimately led to the refinery's permanent closure and the loss of more than a thousand jobs. And on"},{"t":209.6,"txt":"April 26th, 2018, a massive explosion at the Husky refinery in Superior, Wisconsin, sent"},{"t":216.1,"txt":"metal debris flying through the air. One metal fragment punctured a nearby asphalt storage tank,"},{"t":222.9,"txt":"allowing 17,000 barrels of hot asphalt to spill from the tank and ignite, causing multiple fires."},{"t":230.8,"txt":"Roughly 39,000 lbs of flammable hydrocarbon vapor were released into the air. 36 people"},{"t":238.2,"txt":"were injured by the incident and there was over $550 million of property damage at the facility."},{"t":245.1,"txt":"More than 2500 residents of Superior were evacuated from their homes. The CSB found that"},{"t":252.2,"txt":"these incidents all had one thing in common. They were completely preventable. In response to the"},{"t":259,"txt":"many incidents investigated by the CSB, the agency has issued over 1,000 safety recommendations to"},{"t":266,"txt":"make the chemical industry safer while maintaining economic productivity, protecting jobs,"},{"t":272,"txt":"and keeping workers and communities safe. Strong safety programs are critical for the economic"},{"t":279,"txt":"success of our nation's chemical industry and for the health and safety of the people who work in"},{"t":284.2,"txt":"chemical facilities and those who live near them. If the CSB's many safety lessons have prevented"},{"t":290.2,"txt":"at least one catastrophic chemical incident, the money saved by protecting lives, preventing"},{"t":296.1,"txt":"serious injuries and damage to facilities, safeguarding surrounding communities, and"},{"t":301.2,"txt":"avoiding costly litigation and legal settlements, far exceeds the CSB's modest annual budget. The"},{"t":308,"txt":"CSB more than pays for itself and costs saved by preventing serious chemical incidents."}],"words":[{"t":1.207,"w":"The"},{"t":1.207,"w":"US"},{"t":1.207,"w":"Chemical"},{"t":1.207,"w":"Safety"},{"t":1.207,"w":"Board"},{"t":1.207,"w":"is"},{"t":1.207,"w":"an"},{"t":1.207,"w":"independent"},{"t":1.207,"w":"non-regulatory"},{"t":1.207,"w":"agency"},{"t":1.207,"w":"created"},{"t":1.207,"w":"by"},{"t":7.52,"w":"Congress"},{"t":7.52,"w":"that"},{"t":7.52,"w":"investigates"},{"t":7.52,"w":"catastrophic"},{"t":7.52,"w":"chemical"},{"t":7.52,"w":"incidents"},{"t":7.52,"w":"and"},{"t":7.52,"w":"makes"},{"t":7.52,"w":"rec